  "evidence": "TEXT_D: Zero NHS waiting lists using independent capacity and staff incentives; raise income tax threshold to £20,000; scrap energy levies and VAT on energy; 20p fuel duty cut; strong immigration controls (freeze non‑essential immigration, detain/remove illegal arrivals, restrict student dependants); recruit 40,000 police and tougher sentencing; fast‑track brownfield housing. TEXT_C: Large green transition and public ownership programme; no new oil and gas and a rising carbon tax up to ~£500/t; stop new roads, develop road pricing, end ICE sales by 2027; major constitutional changes (PR, elected second chamber), route to rejoin the EU; extensive wealth and business tax reforms; very high public spending across services.",
  "reasoning": "Voters’ top concerns typically center on the NHS, cost of living/taxes and energy bills, immigration, crime, and housing, which TEXT_D addresses directly with immediate, concrete measures on waiting times, tax/energy bill cuts, border enforcement, policing, and housing. TEXT_C prioritizes expansive climate/structural reforms, new taxes (including carbon tax and road pricing), and constitutional changes that are more ideological and less closely tied to the median voter’s immediate priorities.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_D"
}
